# Slay Idle Repeat — optional local overrides for the compose stack.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # only if you need to; the stack works without it
#
# ============================================================================
# READ THIS FIRST: YOU DO NOT NEED THIS FILE.
#
# `docker compose up -d --wait` works on a clean checkout with no .env at all.
# Every value below already has the same default baked into docker-compose.yml
# via `${NAME:-default}`, so this file exists for exactly one reason: HOST PORT
# COLLISIONS. If you already run a Postgres on 5432 or a Grafana on 3000, copy
# this to .env and change the number.
#
# `.env` is gitignored. `.env.example` is committed. Nothing in either is a
# secret, and nothing that IS a secret may ever be added to either.
# ============================================================================
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why there are no credentials here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 14 §1.1 🔒: "`docker compose up` brings the entire stack ... up on a laptop
# with no cloud account", and "Secrets | Injected as environment variables; the
# platform's secret store is an implementation detail of deployment, not of the
# app."
#
# The dev credentials for Postgres, MinIO and Grafana are written in plain sight
# in docker-compose.yml, next to the services that use them. That is on purpose:
#
#   * a value in a committed compose file is obviously a throwaway for a
#     container on a laptop, and cannot be mistaken for a real secret;
#   * putting them here instead would create a file people copy, edit, and then
#     one day paste a real production password into;
#   * anything a deployed environment actually needs is injected by that
#     environment, and never read from a file in this repository.
#
# build/ci/Test-NoCloudCredentials.ps1 enforces the rest: no `secrets.*`
# injection, no registry login, no private cloud registry, no CI templating in
# the compose file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host ports. Container-side ports never change — only the left-hand side.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# SlayIdleRepeat.Server. ⚠️ The compose-boot CI job and
# build/ci/Wait-ForHttpOk.ps1 poll http://127.0.0.1:8080/health. Change this
# locally if you must; do not change the default.
SIR_API_PORT=8080

# Postgres — the usual suspect for a collision.
SIR_POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# Redis.
SIR_REDIS_PORT=6379

# MinIO: S3 API, then the web console.
SIR_MINIO_API_PORT=9000
SIR_MINIO_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# OTLP ingest on the collector. Published so a server started with `dotnet run`
# on the host (rather than in the stack) can still export telemetry.
SIR_OTLP_GRPC_PORT=4317
SIR_OTLP_HTTP_PORT=4318

# Jaeger UI.
SIR_JAEGER_UI_PORT=16686

# Prometheus.
SIR_PROMETHEUS_PORT=9090

# Grafana.
SIR_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
